          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-644bf71bebd716da\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.87,\"analysis\":\"Rafa’s “Come ti amo, Loretta!” (“How I love you, Loretta!”) strongly avows the extent of his loving state. Its immediate emergence in response to Loretta’s hint of reciprocal attraction, together with the narrator’s description of his ingenuous exclamation, gives moderate independent support to expressive discharge. It does not itself undertake a commitment or obligation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The exclamative construction and ingenuous, immediate response suggest E, but they do not conclusively distinguish an affect-driven discharge from an emphatically delivered T avowal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rafa directly addresses Loretta with the exclamative avowal “Come ti amo, Loretta!”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rafa has already repeatedly described his love and pressed Loretta not to end their meetings. When she hints that she may herself be in danger of no longer treating the affair lightly, he immediately answers with this exclamation and puts an arm around her waist. The utterance therefore both reaffirms his established emotional claim and reacts affectively to apparent encouragement. Although the wider conversation concerns continuing their relationship, these words contain no distinct promise or undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Come ti amo, Loretta!”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense love predication, intensified by the exclamative “Come,” strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“egli esclamò ingenuamente”\",\"supports\":\"The narrator presents the delivery as an ingenuous exclamation, supporting some expressive/reflexive force rather than calculation alone.\",\"confidence\":0.85},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rafa exclaims immediately after Loretta suggests that she too may be afraid of ceasing to laugh about his advances.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is an affective response to perceived reciprocation, adding support for E while remaining compatible with an emphatic T avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Rafa says he has fallen foolishly in love, that Loretta occupies his thoughts all day, and that he cannot give her up.\",\"supports\":\"The sustained surrounding avowals independently reinforce the truth-conditional presentation of a loving emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal, while moderate E captures the plausible affective outburst indicated by its form, timing, and narrative framing. No important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
